Lower the MIPS o32 nan2008 minimum kernel from 4.5.0 to 4.4.0.

glibc pins a 4.5.0 minimum kernel for the o32 nan2008/fp64 ABI because mainline didn't
guarantee correct FR=1/FP64 register context handling until ~4.5. It's a HARD floor: the
effective minimum is max(--enable-kernel, arch_minimum_kernel), so our explicit
--enable-kernel=4.4.0 (CT_GLIBC_MIN_KERNEL) is overridden back up to 4.5.0 unless this floor is
lowered. glibc has no flag to force the minimum below the floor — by design; patching is the
sanctioned escape hatch, and it's exactly what Ingenic did to ship glibc 2.29/nan2008 on the
device's 4.4 kernel.

The Ender 3 V3 KE's Ingenic X2000 runs a vendor 4.4.94 kernel that backports the FR=1/FP64
support, so the 4.5 floor is wrong for this hardware. VERIFIED on-device — toolchain/tests/
fp64test.c, built with this toolchain's default flags, produces correct 2008 NaNs and
bit-identical fp64 results under thread contention on the 4.4.94 kernel (i.e. the kernel really
does save/restore the 64-bit FP registers across context switches). So 4.4.0 is the honest
floor, and it keeps glibc's pre-4.5 syscall fallbacks rather than assuming 4.5+.

(With our 4.14 kernel headers the build no longer FAILS without this patch — 4.14 >= 4.5 clears
glibc's header-version check — but without it the recorded minimum would be 4.5.0, not 4.4.0.
Only the generated `configure` is touched, not configure.ac, to avoid an autoreconf with a
mismatched autoconf version.)

--- a/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/mips/configure
+++ b/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/mips/configure
@@ -473,5 +473,5 @@ libc_cv_gcc_unwind_find_fde=yes
 if test -z "$arch_minimum_kernel"; then
   if test x$libc_cv_mips_nan2008 = xyes; then
-    arch_minimum_kernel=4.5.0
+    arch_minimum_kernel=4.4.0
   fi
 fi
